.TH SLL 3 "October 2026" "" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
sll_init, sll_free, sll_append, sll_prepend, sll_remove_head, sll_contains, sll_get_head, sll_size, sll_is_empty \- singly linked list of generic pointers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include \(dqsll.h\(dq
.PP
.BI "single_linked_list_t *sll_init(int (*" compare ")(void *" x ", void *" y ));
.BI "void sll_free(single_linked_list_t *" list );
.PP
.BI "int sll_append(void *" x ", single_linked_list_t *" list );
.BI "int sll_prepend(void *" x ", single_linked_list_t *" list );
.PP
.BI "void *sll_remove_head(single_linked_list_t *" list );
.BI "void *sll_get_head(single_linked_list_t *" list );
.PP
.BI "bool sll_contains(void *" x ", single_linked_list_t *" list );
.BI "size_t sll_size(single_linked_list_t *" list );
.BI "bool sll_is_empty(single_linked_list_t *" list );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions implement a singly linked list that stores
.I void\ *
elements.
The list type,
.IR single_linked_list_t ,
is opaque; it can only be manipulated through the functions below.
.PP
The list stores the pointers it is given and never copies or frees the data
they point to.
The caller owns the data and must keep it valid for as long as it is in the list.
.PP
.BR sll_init ()
allocates and returns a new, empty list.
.I compare
must not be NULL.
It is used by
.BR sll_contains ()
to test elements for equality.
It is called as
.IR compare(x,\ element)
and must return 0 when the two are considered equal.
.PP
.BR sll_free ()
frees every node in the list and the list itself.
The data pointers stored in the nodes are
.B not
freed.
.PP
.BR sll_append ()
adds
.I x
to the end of the list.
This requires walking the whole list, so it takes O(n) time.
.PP
.BR sll_prepend ()
adds
.I x
to the front of the list in O(1) time.
.PP
.BR sll_remove_head ()
removes the first node of the list, frees the node, and returns the data pointer
it held.
.PP
.BR sll_contains ()
returns true if
.I compare
returns 0 for any element in the list, checking from head to tail.
.PP
.BR sll_get_head ()
returns the data pointer stored in the first node without removing it.
The list must not be empty; see
.BR BUGS .
.PP
.BR sll_size ()
returns the number of elements in the list.
.PP
.BR sll_is_empty ()
returns true if the list has no elements.
.SH RETURN VALUE
.BR sll_init ()
returns a pointer to the new list, or NULL if
.I compare
is NULL or memory allocation fails.
.PP
.BR sll_append ()
and
.BR sll_prepend ()
return 0 on success and \-1 on failure.
Failure occurs if
.I x
or
.I list
is NULL, or if memory allocation fails.
Because NULL is rejected, NULL cannot be stored in the list.
.PP
.BR sll_remove_head ()
returns the removed data pointer, or NULL if the list is empty.
.PP
.BR sll_contains ()
returns true if a match is found and false otherwise (including when the list is empty).
.PP
.BR sll_get_head ()
returns the data pointer of the first element.
The behavior is undefined if the list is empty.
.PP
.BR sll_size ()
returns the element count.
.PP
.BR sll_is_empty ()
returns true if the list is empty.
.SH NOTES
Except for
.BR sll_append ()
and
.BR sll_prepend (),
the functions do not check for a NULL
.I list
argument.
Passing NULL results in undefined behavior.
.PP
The functions are not thread-safe.
Concurrent access to the same list requires external locking.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include \(dqsll.h\(dq

static int cmp_str(void *x, void *y)
{
    return strcmp(x, y);
}

int main(void)
{
    single_linked_list_t *list = sll_init(cmp_str);
    if (!list)
        return 1;

    sll_append(\(dqworld\(dq, list);
    sll_prepend(\(dqhello\(dq, list);

    if (sll_contains(\(dqworld\(dq, list))
        printf(\(dqfound, size = %zu\en\(dq, sll_size(list));

    while (!sll_is_empty(list))
        printf(\(dq%s\en\(dq, (char *) sll_remove_head(list));

    sll_free(list);
    return 0;
}
.fi
.SH BUGS
.BR sll_get_head ()
does not check whether the list is empty and dereferences the head pointer
unconditionally, so calling it on an empty list crashes.
Call
.BR sll_is_empty ()
first.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR malloc (3),
.BR free (3)
